  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1105 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nCurrent radar shows the snow for the high country only lingering\nin the southern third of Park County. Some additional snow of \n1-2\" will be possible there over the next few hours as the upper- \nlevel low shifts off to the east. We expect the Winter Weather \nAdvisory to be allowed to expire at 2 AM Thursday. Some light \nrain is also ongoing in far southern Lincoln County. A few more \nscattered rain showers remain possible there, but this chance \nquickly ends by 5 AM this morning. Lows tonight are forecast to be\nsimilar to last night with temperatures near or just above \nfreezing for the plains and in the upper teens to 20s for the high\ncountry. Thursday should be a pleasant day as a ridge aloft \nbriefly builds in. We expect mostly sunny skies and high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 50s for the plains.\n\nThe trough on Friday has trended a bit further south since last \nnight, but moisture still looks minimal along with poor lift. Most\nshort-term guidance is showing at least some QPF for the central \nand northern high country. Thus, Friday afternoon and evening now \nhas the chance for some light mountain snow above 8,500 ft. This \nforecast remains in line with the previous package. For locations\nin the high country mainly north of I-70, we are forecasting 2 \ninches of snow or less with perhaps a few locations approaching 3 \ninches. REFS DESI probabilities show the chance for any locations\nin our area to receive >2 inches to be around 30% or less. RMNP, \nthe Mummy Range, the Indian Peaks, and the Never Summer Mountains \nare most favored for the \"higher\" totals. Model soundings show \nthese locations could have a small amount of weak upslope. Some \nlight widely scattered rain showers could spread into Weld County \nalong and west of I-25, but there is only a 15% chance or less. \nOtherwise, expect highs on Friday in the mid to upper 50s for the \nplains with a slight increase in clouds compared to the last few \ndays.\n\nFrom Saturday through at least Wednesday, ridging aloft will be \nfirmly in place for our area. Above average temperatures are likely \nto prevail under mostly sunny skies. Ensemble guidance continues to \nhone in on Sunday and Monday as the warmest days of the week with \nhigh temperatures in the mid to upper 60s for the urban corridor and \nhighs near 70 F on the far eastern plains. High temperatures look to \nstay in the low to mid 60s from Wednesday onward as the ridge shifts \na bit to the south. Any precipitation looks highly unlikely after\nFriday through at least the end of the forecast period.",
